#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:142
msgid "parseInteger(str, options)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:144
msgid ""
"Parses the contents of `str` (a string) as an integer. On success, the "
"integer value is returned (as a number). On failure, an error is "
"**returned** describing why parsing failed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:148
msgid ""
"By default, leading and trailing whitespace characters are not allowed, "
"nor are trailing characters that are not part of the numeric "
"representation. This behaviour can be toggled by using the options below."
" The empty string (`''`) is not considered valid input. If the return "
"value cannot be precisely represented as a number (i.e., is smaller than "
"`Number.MIN_SAFE_INTEGER` or larger than `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`), an "
"error is returned. Additionally, the string `'-0'` will be parsed as the "
"integer `0`, instead of as the IEEE floating point value `-0`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:157
msgid ""
"This function accepts both upper and lowercase characters for digits, "
"similar to `parseInt()`, `Number()`, and "
"[strtol(3C)](https://illumos.org/man/3C/strtol)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:160
msgid "The following may be specified in `options`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "Option"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "Type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "Default"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "Meaning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "base"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "number"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "10"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "numeric base (radix) to use, in the range 2 to 36"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "allowSign"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "boolean"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "true"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid ""
"whether to interpret any leading `+` (positive) and `-` (negative) "
"characters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "allowImprecise"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "false"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid ""
"whether to accept values that may have lost precision (past "
"`MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` or below `MIN_SAFE_INTEGER`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "allowPrefix"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid ""
"whether to interpret the prefixes `0b` (base 2), `0o` (base 8), `0t` "
"(base 10), or `0x` (base 16)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "allowTrailing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "whether to ignore trailing characters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "trimWhitespace"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "whether to trim any leading or trailing whitespace/line terminators"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "leadingZeroIsOctal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md
msgid "whether a leading zero indicates octal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:172
msgid ""
"Note that if `base` is unspecified, and `allowPrefix` or "
"`leadingZeroIsOctal` are, then the leading characters can change the "
"default base from 10. If `base` is explicitly specified and `allowPrefix`"
" is true, then the prefix will only be accepted if it matches the "
"specified base. `base` and `leadingZeroIsOctal` cannot be used together."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:178
msgid ""
"**Context:** It's tricky to parse integers with JavaScript's built-in "
"facilities for several reasons:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:181
msgid ""
"`parseInt()` and `Number()` by default allow the base to be specified in "
"the input string by a prefix (e.g., `0x` for hex)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:183
msgid "`parseInt()` allows trailing nonnumeric characters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:184
msgid "`Number(str)` returns 0 when `str` is the empty string (`''`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:185
msgid ""
"Both functions return incorrect values when the input string represents a"
" valid integer outside the range of integers that can be represented "
"precisely. Specifically, `parseInt('9007199254740993')` returns "
"9007199254740992."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:188
msgid "Both functions always accept `-` and `+` signs before the digit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:189
msgid ""
"Some older JavaScript engines always interpret a leading 0 as indicating "
"octal, which can be surprising when parsing input from users who expect a"
" leading zero to be insignificant."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:193
msgid ""
"While each of these may be desirable in some contexts, there are also "
"times when none of them are wanted. `parseInteger()` grants greater "
"control over what input's permissible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:197
msgid "iso8601(date)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:199
msgid ""
"Converts a Date object to an ISO8601 date string of the form \"YYYY-MM-"
"DDTHH:MM:SS.sssZ\".  This format is not customizable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:203
msgid "parseDateTime(str)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:205
msgid ""
"Parses a date expressed as a string, as either a number of milliseconds "
"since the epoch or any string format that Date accepts, giving preference"
" to the former where these two sets overlap (e.g., strings containing "
"small numbers)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:210
msgid "hrtimeDiff(timeA, timeB)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:212
msgid ""
"Given two hrtime readings (as from Node's `process.hrtime()`), where "
"timeA is later than timeB, compute the difference and return that as an "
"hrtime.  It is illegal to invoke this for a pair of times where timeB is "
"newer than timeA."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:216
msgid "hrtimeAdd(timeA, timeB)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:218
msgid ""
"Add two hrtime intervals (as from Node's `process.hrtime()`), returning a"
" new hrtime interval array.  This function does not modify either input "
"argument."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:222
msgid "hrtimeAccum(timeA, timeB)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:224
msgid ""
"Add two hrtime intervals (as from Node's `process.hrtime()`), storing the"
" result in `timeA`.  This function overwrites (and returns) the first "
"argument passed in."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:229
msgid "hrtimeNanosec(timeA), hrtimeMicrosec(timeA), hrtimeMillisec(timeA)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:231
msgid ""
"This suite of functions converts a hrtime interval (as from Node's "
"`process.hrtime()`) into a scalar number of nanoseconds, microseconds or "
"milliseconds.  Results are truncated, as with `Math.floor()`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:236
msgid "validateJsonObject(schema, object)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:238
msgid ""
"Uses JSON validation (via JSV) to validate the given object against the "
"given schema.  On success, returns null.  On failure, *returns* (does not"
" throw) a useful Error object."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:243
msgid "extraProperties(object, allowed)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:245
msgid ""
"Check an object for unexpected properties.  Accepts the object to check, "
"and an array of allowed property name strings.  If extra properties are "
"detected, an array of extra property names is returned.  If no properties"
" other than those in the allowed list are present on the object, the "
"returned array will be of zero length."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:251
msgid "mergeObjects(provided, overrides, defaults)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:253
msgid ""
"Merge properties from objects \"provided\", \"overrides\", and "
"\"defaults\".  The intended use case is for functions that accept named "
"arguments in an \"args\" object, but want to provide some default values "
"and override other values.  In that case, \"provided\" is what the caller"
" specified, \"overrides\" are what the function wants to override, and "
"\"defaults\" contains default values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/jsprim_README.md:259
msgid ""
"The function starts with the values in \"defaults\", overrides them with "
"the values in \"provided\", and then overrides those with the values in "
"\"overrides\". For convenience, any of these objects may be falsey, in "
"which case they will be ignored.  The input objects are never modified, "
"but properties in the returned object are not deep-copied."
msgstr ""

